ible flips in convex point sets as shown by Aichholzer, Dorfer, and Vogtenhuber [GD 2025].\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nCounterexamples to the Happy Edge Conjecture in Plane Spanning Trees\n\nWe study the problem of reconfiguring plane, straight-line spanning trees on point sets in the plane. A flip is a reconfiguration step in which one edge of a tree is replaced with another, yielding again a plane spanning tree. For a given point set $P$, the flip graph has a vertex for each plane spanning tree on $P$, and an edge between any two trees that differ by a single flip. The folklore happy edge conjecture suggests that any edge shared by both the initial and the target tree (a happy edge) is never flipped in a shortest flip sequence. This conjecture was computationally verified for up to 10 points in convex position. In this paper, we disprove this conjecture both on general point sets and on point sets in convex position by providing two counterexamples on $16$ and $30$ points, respectively. The counterexample for point sets in general position also holds for compatible flips, while the happy edge property holds for compatible flips in convex point sets as shown by Aichholzer, Dorfer, and Vogtenhuber [GD 2025].\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe weak happy edge conjecture for plane spanning trees — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/287755/the-weak-happy-edge-conjecture-for-plane-spanning-trees)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The weak happy edge conjecture for plane spanning trees ... Weak happy edge conjecture.\n\n# \n\nThe weak happy edge conjecture for plane spanning trees\n\nConjecture open\n\nLet $S$S be a convex point set, and let $T_{\\text{in}}$T in​ and $T_{\\text{tar}}$T tar​ be plane spanning trees on $S$S. An edge is happy if it belongs to both $T_{\\text{in}}$T in​ and $T_{\\text{tar}}$T tar​. Weak happy edge conjecture. There is a shortest flip sequence from $T_{\\text{in}}$T in​ to $T_{\\text{tar}}$T tar​ that does not flip happy edges. This conjecture asks whether shortest reconfiguration sequences can preserve all edges common to the initial and target trees. Its resolution is not given in the supplied text.\n\n## References\n\nSource: https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15520 Aichholzer et al. (2024), cited in the source as `aichholzer2024reconfiguration`\n\nStatus: no resolution is reported in the source (as of August 2025).\n\nflip-graphsgeometric-reconfigurationplane-spanning-trees\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 3 days ago source: arXiv:2508.15520--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMonotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02108)\nciteturn7academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Title: Monotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs ... Date: Mon Aug  4 06:37:13 2025\nTitle: Monotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs\nAuthors: François Clément, Dan Guyer\nDate: Mon Aug  4 06:37:13 2025\n\nMotivated by trying to understand the behavior of the simplex method, Athanasiadis, De Loera and Zhang provided upper and lower bounds on the number of the monotone paths on 3-polytopes. For simple 3-polytopes with $2n$ vertices, they showed that the number of monotone paths is bounded above by $(1+\\varphi)^n$, with $\\varphi$ being the golden ratio. We improve the result and show that for a larger family of graphs the number is bounded above by $c \\cdot 1.6779^n$ for some universal constant $c$. Meanwhile, the best known construction and conjectured extremizer is approximately $\\varphi^n$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMonotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2508.02108)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Monotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs ... @misc{clément2025monotonepathsacyclic3regular, title={Monotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs}, author={François Clément and Dan Guyer}, year={2025}, eprint={2508.02108}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02108}, }\nSubmitted 04 Aug 2025\n\n# Monotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs\n\nFC\n\nFrançois Clément\n\nDG\n\nDan Guyer\n\n## Abstract\n\nMotivated by trying to understand the behavior of the simplex method, Athanasiadis, De Loera and Zhang provided upper and lower bounds on the number of the monotone paths on 3-polytopes. For simple 3-polytopes with $2n$2 n vertices, they showed that the number of monotone paths is bounded above by $(1+\\varphi)^n$(1+φ)n, with $\\varphi$φ being the golden ratio. We improve the result and show that for a larger family of graphs the number is bounded above by $c \\cdot 1.6779^n$c⋅1.677 9 n for some universal constant $c$c. Meanwhile, the best known construction and conjectured extremizer is approximately $\\varphi^n$φ n.\n\nView Paper\n\nView PDF\n\n1 views\n\nv1\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{clément2025monotonepathsacyclic3regular, title={Monotone Paths on Acyclic 3-Regular Graphs}, author={François Clément and Dan Guyer}, year={2025}, eprint={2508.02108}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02108}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstrained Flips in Plane Spanning Trees (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol357-gd2025/LIPIcs.GD.2025.5/LIPIcs.GD.2025.5.pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; (weak) happy edge property, if, from any initial graph Gin to any target graph Gtar, there ... In [1], the authors formulated the Weak Happy Edge Conjecture for trees on convex point\nedge at a time.\n(i) If any flip sequence in P that flips at least one happy edge can be shortened by at least\ntwo flips, then P fulfills the perfect flip property.\n(ii) If for any flip in P from G to (G \\ {e1}) ∪{e2}, e1 and e2 can never be in